\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What replaced what.}
The original claim was factor blindness \emph{with a caveat}: a reading of a few
hundredths of a bit, statistically indistinguishable from zero. The theory supplies a
different kind of statement. The leakage is $0$ exactly, on every swap-closed,
off-diagonal population, for every symmetric readout, at every chain width --- with no
sample, no null and no sensitivity floor. The empirical residual is fully accounted for:
Theorem~\ref{thm:nonneg} says the estimator cannot go below the truth, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:sparse} says that in the observed regime it sits at the label entropy
regardless of dependence.

\paragraph{Methodological morals.}
(1) One-sided statistics need one-sided scepticism: a small positive plug-in reading is
the default, not evidence. (2) A degenerate null is a red flag, not a precision claim: a
null standard deviation of $0.0014$ bits over $200$ shuffles means the statistic is nearly
invariant under relabelling, so the $z$-score is a ratio of two vanishing quantities.
(3) Symmetry beats sampling: one structural observation settles in a line what thousands
of samples could only bound. (4) Verify the exact object: during the verification a first
pass chained only two of the four fields, testing a smaller cousin of the flagged device;
it passed, correctly but about the wrong object.

\paragraph{Design consequences.}
Which-factor leakage is decided entirely by the symmetry class of the readout, not by the
width of the chain or the size of the sample. If leakage is wanted, the readout must break
the symmetry explicitly --- and by Theorem~\ref{thm:power} even the crudest asymmetry is
detected at full strength. If blindness is wanted, routing through elementary symmetric
functions guarantees it exactly.

\paragraph{Scope and limitations.}
The theorems concern the exact empirical law of a finite population, the right object when
the population is enumerable. They do not bound the leakage of a \emph{biased sample};
indeed the sparse analysis shows a sample can read near the maximum. The sparse closed
form assumes exact injectivity of the code on the sample; with collisions the reading is
only bounded by the label entropy and the null acquires a small spread, as the numerics
show. Finally, the perfect-square exception is a genuine positive reading, even though its
source is a label-degenerate diagonal cell rather than an arithmetic channel.

\section{Future directions}

\subsection*{A. The coset leakage law for partially symmetric readouts}

\begin{conjecture}
Let a finite group $G$ act on a population $S$ with a torsor label $\lab : S\to L$, and let
the readout $c$ be invariant only under a subgroup $H\le G$. Then
\[
  0 \;\le\; I(\lab; c) \;\le\; \log_2 [G:H],
\]
with the upper bound attained exactly when the readout separates the $H$-cosets.
\end{conjecture}

The two proved extremes are $H=G$ (leakage $0$, Theorem~\ref{thm:orbit}) and $H=1$ with a
separating readout (a full bit at $|G|=2$, Theorem~\ref{thm:power}). The key insight is
that $H$-invariance already forces every readout fiber to be balanced along $H$-orbits, so
the conditional law of the label given the readout is constant on $H$-cosets: only the
$[G:H]$ coset coordinates can carry information, and a maximum-entropy bound on a
$[G:H]$-letter alphabet finishes the estimate. A real battery is never exactly symmetric
--- rounding, truncation and modulus choice break invariance under part of the group ---
so the conjecture converts ``how symmetric is my readout?'' into a numerical leakage
budget. A natural first test builds explicit readouts on the six orderings of a triple
that are invariant under $A_3 \le S_3$ but not under $S_3$, and checks the predicted
ceiling of $1$ bit.

\subsection*{B. Bias-corrected estimators for the sparse regime}

Theorem~\ref{thm:sparse} gives the plug-in reading exactly in the injective limit. The
sequel is a correction: subtract the predicted label entropy, or design a statistic
measurable with respect to the fiber structure rather than the sample identity, and prove
that the corrected statistic has a non-degenerate permutation null.

\subsection*{C. Collision regimes between the two extremes}

With $n$ samples and $K$ effective code values, the reading interpolates between
$H(\lab)$ (sparse, $K\approx n$) and the truth (dense, $K\ll n$). Quantifying the crossover
--- ideally as an exact formula for a random-fiber model --- would predict the observed
gap between a reading of $0.8985$ bits and the ceiling of $1$ bit in terms of the collision
count alone.

\subsection*{D. Beyond torsor labels}

Orbit blindness assumes exactly one group element realises each target label. Labels that
are only equivariant should obey a stabiliser-corrected counting core, with $|G|$ replaced
by an orbit size; this would cover populations of tuples with repeated factors --- the
multi-prime analogue of the perfect-square exception.

\section{Conclusion}

An apparent $0.0469$-bit wall in the which-factor readout of a four-field chained battery
has been resolved completely. The reading was estimator bias: the plug-in mutual
information is nonnegative by construction, and in the near-injective regime it is a
function of the label counts alone, so its permutation null cannot move. The truth is
exactly zero, and the reason is structural: the battery is routed through the trace and
the norm of the factor pair, so it sees only the unordered pair, and no invariant function
separates an orbit. The statement holds for any symmetric readout on any swap-closed,
off-diagonal population; for all ordered factorisations of any non-square integer; for any
finite group with an invariant readout and a torsor label; and, with the symmetric group,
for the entire ordering pattern of any number of pairwise distinct factors. Capacity
remains untouched --- about twenty bits of room for the four-field battery, some fourteen
of them realised --- and all of it is symmetric content. There is no wall in the
which-factor direction, because there is no such direction to measure.

